[
  {
    "file": "ts/slot-card.ts",
    "line": 271,
    "summary": "When a new use arrives, the history is re-read right away, but the recorder has usually not committed the new state yet, so the reloaded list still leaves out the use that triggered the reload.",
    "failure_scenario": "The list is open and Alice unlocks the Front Door. The event entity state changes, the subscription pushes a new last_used, and _loadUses calls history/history_during_period at once. That call reads the database, and the recorder commits in batches every few seconds (commit_interval), so the new row is not there yet. The header says 'Front Door · now' while the list below it stops at the previous use. Nothing loads the list again until the next use, and that reload misses its own use the same way. Re-expanding within a few seconds of a use has the same problem."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/credential-uses.ts",
    "line": 89,
    "summary": "parseCredentialUses lists every recorded state of the event entity ID without checking the `name` or `config_entry_id` attributes, so uses recorded for an earlier owner of that entity ID show up as this user's uses.",
    "failure_scenario": "Alice is removed and a new user named Alice is added in the same entry. The new user gets the same event entity_id (it is built from the name), and the recorder keys history by entity_id. The new Alice's 'Recent uses' then shows the old Alice's uses from the last 30 days, attributed to the new person. The same happens when an entity ID is reassigned by hand. Filtering rows on attributes.name / config_entry_id would rule this out."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/model/events.py",
    "line": 141,
    "summary": "async_log_entry gets no context, so the logbook entry is not linked to the service call or the lock state change behind it.",
    "failure_scenario": "An automation calls lock_code_manager.use_credential. The logbook entry carries a fresh context, so the logbook cannot show 'triggered by automation X'. For a use the lock observed, the entry is not tied to the lock's 'changed to Unlocked' entry it is meant to explain. async_fire_credential_used has no context parameter, and the services.py caller has call.context available and drops it."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/model/events.py",
    "line": 141,
    "summary": "The logbook entry is written for every use, including uses the event entity drops because their kind is not in event_types, so the lock's logbook and the card's 'Recent uses' disagree.",
    "failure_scenario": "A provider under-reports fingerprint support and a fingerprint use is fired. _handle_credential_used drops it at DEBUG, so no event entity state (and no history row) is recorded, but async_log_entry has already written 'Alice used a fingerprint to unlock' on the lock. The lock's activity shows the use, while the user's card 'Recent uses' and 'Last used' never show it."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/credential-uses.ts",
    "line": 92,
    "summary": "History rows whose attributes predate the unified payload, or lack `target`, render an empty target name and an empty kind.",
    "failure_scenario": "Within the 30-day window there are rows recorded by an older event-entity attribute shape with no `target` (and possibly no credential_type or event_type). target becomes '', so targetName is '' and the use-target span is blank. describeCredentialUse returns '' or ' · Unlocked'. The row shows only a relative time and nothing says what it was used against."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/slot-card.ts",
    "line": 529,
    "summary": "_loadUses sets _uses to undefined before every fetch, so each new use (and each re-expand) swaps the list for 'Loading…' and then back.",
    "failure_scenario": "A user whose list is open and who gets frequent uses (a busy front door) sees it flicker to 'Loading…' every time last_used changes. Combined with the commit race above, it then redraws without the newest use. Keeping the previous list until the response arrives avoids the flash."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/credential-uses.ts",
    "line": 39,
    "summary": "CREDENTIAL_LABELS hard-codes English labels that duplicate the event_type state translations already in strings.json, and some differ ('RFID tag' vs 'RFID').",
    "failure_scenario": "The kind label in the card's list is English for every locale and does not match the label HA shows for the same event_type in more-info and history ('RFID' vs 'RFID tag', 'NFC tag'). hass.formatEntityAttributeValue(stateObj, 'event_type', kind), or localize with the entity translation key, would reuse the existing translations. A new kind also needs adding in two places."
  },
  {
