content.content:
Skill "code-review" completed (forked execution).

Result:
I found no high-severity bugs in PR #1796. The Python logbook path is correct for both callers. The real issues are in how the card manages the history stream, plus some cleanup and one place where the PR description and the code disagree. These findings went through a dedup pass only, with no verification step.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "ts/slot-card.ts",
    "line": 566,
    "summary": "The stream callback decides whether a message is current by comparing entity IDs, not by checking which stream sent it. After collapsing and re-expanding on the same user, the closed stream's messages still pass the check and are merged.",
    "failure_scenario": "Expand, collapse before the subscribe promise resolves, then expand again on the same event entity. `_closeUses` only unsubscribes once that promise resolves, so the old stream's history or live message can arrive while `_usesEntityId === eventEntityId`. It passes the guard and is merged into the new `_useRows`. The new list leaves 'Loading…' early, showing whatever the stale stream sent (for example one live row) before its own history arrives. The guard should compare the stream itself (for example `this._usesUnsub === unsub`), not the entity ID."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/slot-card.ts",
    "line": 280,
    "summary": "When the slot comes to name another user, `_handleSubscriptionData` reopens the history stream even if the event row will render static or not at all, and `_usesExpanded` stays true.",
    "failure_scenario": "List open for Alice; the slot is reassigned to Bob, who has `last_used: null`. `_handleSubscriptionData` opens a 30-day `history/stream` for Bob's entity, but `_renderEventRow` returns the static 'Never used' row, which never calls `_renderUses`. A subscription runs with nothing showing it. When Bob first uses a credential, the row appears already expanded, without the user having clicked it. The same happens when the event entity goes `unavailable`: the row returns `nothing` but the stream stays open."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/slot-card.ts",
    "line": 562,
    "summary": "If `_openUses` returns early, `_usesExpanded` stays true with `_useRows` undefined, so the list shows 'Loading…' forever. The entity-change check in `_handleSubscriptionData` then closes and reopens the stream on every slot push.",
    "failure_scenario": "Either `connection.subscribeMessage` is missing, or `event_entity_id` is null in a slot update while the list is open. `_usesEntityId` stays undefined, so `null !== undefined` is true on every `_handleSubscriptionData` call, and each push runs `_closeUses` then `_openUses` again. If the row renders, it is stuck on 'Loading…' with no error state, because `_useRows` is never set to null."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/slot-card.ts",
    "line": 578,
    "summary": "The PR description says 're-expanding reuses the rows already read', but `_closeUses` discards `_useRows`, and the DOM test asserts that re-expanding opens a fresh stream with an empty list.",
    "failure_scenario": "Collapse and re-expand: `_closeUses` sets `_useRows = undefined`, so the card shows 'Loading…' and re-downloads 30 days of history. The release notes and reviewers are told the opposite. Either the description or the code needs to change."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/credential-uses.ts",
    "line": 66,
    "summary": "`significant_changes_only: false` streams every attribute-only write of the event entity for 30 days, with attributes. Every use changes the entity's state (its timestamp), so `true` would still deliver every use.",
    "failure_scenario": "The code's own comment says `event_types` attribute writes happen 'as locks are probed'. Each of those writes is sent with full attributes and then discarded by `mergeUseRows`. The same cost is paid again on every expand and on every websocket resubscribe after a reconnect, even though the card keeps only 10 rows. Setting `significant_changes_only: true` sends only rows where the state changed. That includes every use and the restart rewrite that dedup already handles."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/credential-uses.ts",
    "line": 116,
    "summary": "`parseCredentialUses` repeats the filter, dedup-by-time, sort and slice that `mergeUseRows` already guarantees, and the card wraps `_useRows` in a dummy `HistoryResponse` only so the parser can unwrap it.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every render runs `isUse`, Map dedup, sort and `slice(CREDENTIAL_USE_LIMIT)` a second time over rows that are already deduplicated, sorted and capped. The two copies can drift, for example if the limit or the dedup key changes in one place only. Simpler form: `mergeUseRows` owns normalization, and a `toCredentialUse(row, states)` mapper takes `CompressedState[]` directly."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/model/events.py",
    "line": 91,
    "summary": "`credential_use_message` indexes `_CREDENTIAL_PHRASES[credential_type]` directly. A credential type with no phrase raises `KeyError` inside the provider's callback, after the bus event has already fired.",
    "failure_scenario": "Add a `CredentialType` member without updating `_CREDENTIAL_PHRASES`. Every lock-observed use of that kind raises from `async_fire_code_slot_event`, inside the provider's push handler, so the logbook entry is lost. The CI test catches this only if it runs. Falling back to `.get(credential_type, str(credential_type))` would keep the user-facing path from failing."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/credential-uses.ts",
    "line": 27,
    "summary": "A code comment uses an unexpanded acronym, which breaks the project's comment style rule.",
    "failure_scenario": "Repo `CLAUDE.md` defers to `AGENTS.md`, whose Code Style section says: \"**Spell out acronyms** in code comments instead of using abbreviations.\" The new comment reads `/** When the use was recorded (ISO timestamp, the event entity's state) */`, and 'ISO' (International Organization for Standardization) is not spelled out."
  }
]
```

